WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.980
Ursula has a really clean line

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.840
in everything that she ever wrote.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.180
And I admire this because one of the things it takes is

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.640
focus on what is your story really about.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.420
So if you're writing a novel,

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.940
there's two years that are passing.
You're reading about it, doing research for it.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.460
You learn lots of stories.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.580
What happens to me is, well, 
all these stories are so great.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.700
I have to put them all in.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.580
And I go into an obsessive fit.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.460
I'm thinking that's so interesting,
I can't possibly not include it.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.420
And so is this and so is that.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.680
So my novels kind of like, they begin to... bulge.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.560
As I want to tell every great story that ever occurs to me

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.700
during the two years of writing the novel.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.240
It's something I can't control.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.380
Ursula would know what the story needed to tell you

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.700
in order to make the maximum effect for

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:51.840
however long it was.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.820
So her novels aren't very long.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.960
And they're in proportion to how long they are because she made them as long as they needed to be.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.320
Let me read a sentence to you...

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:10.520
This is a little section, just to give you a sense of it.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.900
"It is always the Year One here.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.700
Only the dating of every past and future year
changes each New Year's Day,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.420
as one counts backwards or forwards
from the unitary Now.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.900
So it was spring of the Year One in Erhenrang,
capital city of Karhide,

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.040
and I was in peril of my life, and did not know it.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:36.020
I was in a parade. I walked just behind
the gossiwors and just before the king.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:47.460
It was raining. Rainclouds over dark towers, rain falling in deep streets, a dark storm-beaten city of stone,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.800
through which one vein of gold winds slowly."

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.840
It's that image you can just see it.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.180
And I think her sentences—this comes from the very first pages of The Left Hand of Darkness—

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.040
it struck me at the time and I've never forgotten it.
I could find it very quickly

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.000
when I was putting this talk together.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.720
Her sentences are like that line of gold

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.440
they run through the thing.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.240
And they are a kind of living wire of thought

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.520
that you can follow when you read it.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.700
That I think is really part of her talent as a writer.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:28.120
And then of course the content: she's strange,
but she's not too strange to understand.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.780
And she again keeps the focus on the main point.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:37.340
So most of her stories have one strange thing
and everything else is more familiar to you.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:41.640
So you can accept it.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.660
That's what makes her available to so many readers

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.700
almost any reader who enjoys any kind of fiction

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.300
can actually understand Le Guin. You don't have to be 
a specialist in science fiction or know

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.980
the history of science fiction. She can indeed
be your entry drug for that reason.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.800
There's also a playfulness to her.
A playfulness and a sense of humor

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.840
even in her heaviest stories.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.140
And maybe it's not true say of "Tehanu," but

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.780
for every one other of her novels no matter
how much she is basically a serious writer

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:17.300
there's a playfulness in the prose and in 
the talk between the characters.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:25.870
And I wanted this picture up here to kind of convey that. That's a quality in her writing, right there.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.580
So there's something that you can only
put together as a kind of a gestalt that

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.600
has to do with intelligence and creativity.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.240
A kind of a vision. A warmth. The warmth is important.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.760
And I'm gonna come back to that. And it adds up to something that we often call wisdom

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.340
or greatness. I prefer the word greatness.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.460
Because who knows what wisdom is in this world,

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.640
but the great artists have some quality that actually

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.800
as you might be able to tell from what I'm saying now

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.120
you can't quite put your finger on it.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.200
But you know it's there when 
you've read enough of that writer.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.820
It doesn't mean that they are the finest stylists or the most poetical. In fact

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:13.000
that is often the surfer that's doing too many turns.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.420
This more has to do with the content
of what she's saying.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.700
And it doesn't have to do with being deadly serious.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.580
I don't want to even name the writers who try to

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.220
convince you that they're important
by their deadly seriousness.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.500
That isn't what she would do.

